## Background

The Stroop effect is a cognitive interference effect from the Stroop task [@stroop_studies_1935], whereby identification of a written colour word’s ink colour is slower when the written word is printed in a conflicting colour: for example, the word ‘blue’ printed in green ink is more slowly identified than when it is printed in blue link, due to interference from the automatic process of reading during colour identification. Subsequent research has led to several variations of the Stroop task. One such variant is the emotional Stroop task, in which participants identify the ink colour of emotionally salient and neutral words rather than colour words. The resulting interference effect is theorised to be the result of interference caused by the emotion stimuli, the process for which is automatic and immediate \[@bargh_automaticity_1997\]. In addition, the Stroop paradigm has been widely applied in bilingual contexts to examine patterns of interference across languages \[@magiste_stroop_1984\]. In bilinguals, research often reflects that the mother tongue (L1) is more strongly associated with emotion, while the second language (L2) can be used to create emotional distance [@dewaele_emotions_2010]. @articlesutton_automatic_2007 investigated the bilingual emotional Stroop task in Spanish bilinguals. They found an emotional Stroop effect, with highly efficient bilinguals displaying similar emotional interference effect in both languages, and concluded that the results reflect that emotional valence of lexical items can be automatically activated in more than one language. [@articlesutton_automatic_2007] had one participant group of English-Spanish early bilinguals, therefore the present study builds on the findings to investigate to what extent age of acquisition influences the emotional interference effect observed in an L1 and L2, in French-English bilingual speakers. The following study builds on the theory of the emotional Stroop effect and bilingual Stroop effect, to investigate the effect of age of acquisition of emotional interference in English-French bilingual speakers. 

### Research Questions/Hypotheses

**Research question:**

The research question asks broadly if age of acquisition influences reaction time to French and English emotion compared to neutral words in a bilingual Stroop paradigm. Specifically, the present study investigates if an emotional interference effect is observed similarly or differentially across early and late bilinguals groups in an emotional bilingual Stroop paradigm across languages (French compared to English). 

**Research hypotheses:** Hypotheses is based on the results of @sutton_automatic_2007.

-   **Hypothesis 1:** Overall, we can predict that both groups will show a slower reaction time in the emotion condition compared to the neutral condition, due to the emotional interference effect.

-   **Hypothesis 2:** In early bilinguals, since they have fluency in both French and English and acquired both languages in early childhood, it is predicted that this group will display an equal amount of emotional interference across both languages, meaning similar reaction times will be shown.

-   **Hypothesis 3:** For late bilinguals, a slower reaction time is predicted for emotion words than for neutral words only in the English condition, since the emotional interference effect is predicted to be stronger in the L1.

Overall, we can expect the emotional interference effect to negatively correlate with age of acquisition, such that early bilinguals will show faster reaction times than late bilinguals to French emotional stimuli.

## Methods

### Participants

The study uses two participants groups: early sequential and simultaneous bilingual French-English speakers (attainment of both languages before the age of 11) and late sequential French-English speakers (native English speakers who learnt French as a second language after the age of 11). The late bilingual group had 6 participants, and the early bilingual groups had 5 participants, so a total of 11 participants took part in the study. 

In the late bilingual group, acquisition of French was between the ages of 11 - 15 (mean = 11), with fluency (mean = 71% range = 50 - 90%) achieved from the ages of 16 - 20 (mean = 18), with one participant describing themselves as not fluent in French. All participants had proficiency and/or fluency additionally in Italian, Spanish, German, and BSL or a combination of these languages. 1 participant had acquired Italian and English from birth, and had acquired L4 French after L3 German, while the remaining 5 participants had acquired French as an L2. On average, participants were exposed to English 79.5% of the time, French 8.4% of the time, and other languages 12.1% of the time. 4 participants reported that they would choose to read in English over another language, while 1 participant reported a 50% preference for French and English, and 1 participant reported that they would choose to read in Italian. 

In the early bilingual group, 4 participants were simultaneous learners of French and English, meaning they acquired them simultaneously from birth, while 1 participant acquired French from birth and English from age 4, and 1 participant was a native French speaker who acquired fluency in English from age 10. All participants had proficiency and/or fluency additionally in Spanish, German and Russian, or a combination of these languages. On average, participants were exposed to English 73.3% and to French 23% of the time, and to another language 3.7% of the time. 4 participants reported that they would choose to read in English, while 1 reported that they would choose to read in French. 

All participants were aged between 19 - 28 and are university educated, and currently reside in the United Kingdom or the United States.

