### **Materials**

***Language history questionnaire***

Firstly, all participants completed a language history questionnaire [@marian_leap-questionnaire_2007], which collects information on their language acquisition background and self-proficiency ratings. The information is presented below in Table 1 (above).

***Stroop task*** 

The Stroop task assessment was built using the materials adapted from @sutton_automatic_2007. In the original study, the word list was composed of two different types of words: negative emotion words (e.g. anger) and neutral (non-emotion related) words which belonged to the category ‘nautical nouns’ (e.g. boat). The English words were repeated in Spanish. @sutton_automatic_2007 highlight that the chosen words were high in arousal and low in valence, and matched for frequency and length. Therefore, the current study uses the same English word list but with French translated equivalents (rather than Spanish) in order to build on the results of @sutton_automatic_2007 and investigate a different language combination. The complete word list (French & English, emotion and neutral items) was repeated in blue ink and in green ink. The word list was therefore divided into three blocks: language (French/English), word type/condition (neutral/emotion) and ink colour (blue/green). Due to the small scale of the study, the translated French words were not matched for frequency and length. 

Participants saw a total of 32 items, as items were counterbalanced across colours and languages: half of the emotion items appeared in English and half in French, and the same applied to neutral items. The items were not repeated across languages (for example, if a participant saw the English word ‘boat’ they would not see the French equivalent ‘bateau’). Items were also counterbalanced across colour: each word was presented once, in one colour, and not repeated in the second colour. In summary, participants saw eight neutral English items (4 in green, 4 in blue), eight emotion English items (4 in green, 4 in blue), eight neutral French items (4 in green, 4 in blue), and eight emotion French items (4 in green, 4 in blue). Items were blocked by language, and the order that the blocks were presented in was pseudorandomised and counterbalanced across participants.

A complete list of stimuli can be found in the Appendix.

### **Procedure**

The Stroop task was self-conducted online via Testable, with each participant completing it on their own device. Participants first completed a practice session, consisting of a total of 6 neutral items, which were in a distinct semantic category to the critical trial neutral items. In the practice condition, the word appeared on the screen for 3 seconds. Participants were instructed to press the ‘L’ key if the word appeared in green ink, and the ‘A’ key if the word appeared in blue ink. Instructions only appeared in English. For the practice trials, the key/colour reminder prompt appeared on the screen alongside the practice word. Participants were not given feedback on the practice condition if their answer was correct. Following the practice condition, the participants immediately completed the critical trial conditions. In the critical condition, the stimuli appeared on the screen for less time - a total of 1.7 seconds - and the prompt did not appear on the screen alongside the stimuli.

### **Statistical Analysis**

In order to answer the research question, *if age of acquisition influences reaction time to French and English emotion compared to neutral words in a bilingual Stroop paradigm,* age of acquisition is treated as a categorical variable of early bilinguals and late bilinguals, and the bilingual nature of the Stroop paradigm is operationalised as language of stimuli (English, French).

Reaction times in milliseconds for each trial will be analysed using mixed-effects regression modelling. Summary measures for mean and standard deviation of the raw data will be reported. The study will then employ a 2x2x2 mixed factorial design of lognormal regression, with group (early vs late) as between-participant fixed effects and condition (emotion vs neutral) and language of stimuli (French vs English) as within-participant random slopes. The interactions between groups and conditions will also be analysed.

### **Data Availability**

Anonymised participant data (Stroop task performance) is available on the following GitHub link: <https://github.com/KatiePedrotti/rmdl-template>

To respect participant privacy, language questionnaires are not available.

## Results: Data analysis

### Reading and processing the data




```{r}
#| label: read-data
library(tidyverse)
library(ggplot2)
library(brms)
library(dplyr)
library(posterior)
library(tidybayes)

```

```{r}
alldata = list.files(
  path = "./data/",
  pattern = "*.csv$",
  recursive = TRUE ,
  full.names = TRUE
  
)

compiledlist <- lapply(alldata, function(x) {
  read_csv(x, skip = 3)
})

finaldata <- bind_rows(compiledlist) |>
  filter(condition1 != "practise") |>
  mutate(
    early_late = case_when(
      substring(id,1,1) == "1"~"early",
      substring(id,1,1) == "2"~"late",
      TRUE ~""
    )
  )
```




As the data were in separate files and lacking identifying columns, I firstly used list.files() to list all the available results files, and lapply() to compile them together, with read_csv(x, skip =3) in order to skip the first 3 rows of each dataset which did not contain any data. Then I used bind_rows() to read in this data into one dataset, filtered out the 6 practise conditions for each participant using filter(), and used mutate() in order to create a column which classified the participants as early or late bilinguals based on their participant number (participants numbers starting with 1 were early bilinguals, and starting with 2 were late bilinguals).




```{r}
newfinaldata <- finaldata |>
  separate_wider_delim(
    condition1,
    delim = "_",
    names = c("Condition", "Language")
  )
```

```{r}
newfinaldata$Language <- recode(newfinaldata$Language,
                             "eng" = "English",
                             "fr"  = "French")

newfinaldata$early_late <- recode(newfinaldata$early_late,
                               "early" = "Early bilingual",
                               "late"  = "Late bilingual")

newfinaldata$Condition <- recode(newfinaldata$Condition,
                               "emotion" = "Emotion",
                               "neutral"  = "Neutral")
```




I used separate_wider_delim() in order to create new columns which identified the condition (emotion and neutral) and language (French and English) (this information had been presented together in one column in the original Testable file, such as emotion_fr), and re-named the column names accordingly. The relevant columns are therefore Language (French/English stimuli), Early_Late (early or late bilingual), and Condition (emotion/neutral stimuli). Importantly, Reaction time (RT) appears in a column, which was captured by Testable.

### Summary of the raw data




```{r}
newfinaldata |> 
  group_by(Condition, Language, early_late) |> 
  summarise(
    mean = mean(RT), sd = sd(RT))
```




In order to investigate if age of acquisition affects reaction time to emotion and neutral words in the bilingual Stroop task, I analysed participant reaction times across condition (neutral vs. emotion) and language (French vs. English) and obtained and standard deviation.

Across conditions, the raw data shows that late bilinguals showed faster mean reaction times than early bilinguals. The results are summarised below and displayed in Figure 1 (below).

In the emotion condition, early bilinguals showed a faster reaction time in French (mean = 726.38ms, SD = 440.26) than in English (mean = 817.83ms, SD = 561.09). This is contrary to the hypothesis, the early bilinguals would perform similarly across languages. The late bilinguals showed the same pattern: faster reaction times in French (mean = 533.33ms, SD = 215.51) than in English (mean = 542.31ms, SD = 255.28). While both groups displayed the same performance pattern, overall in the emotion condition, the late bilinguals had faster reaction times in both languages than the early bilinguals, but the early bilinguals were considerably slower in English, while the variation between language for the late bilinguals was not as pronounced. This is contrary to the hypothesis that early bilinguals will show a faster reaction time than late bilinguals in the emotion condition.

In the neutral condition, early bilinguals had a faster reaction time in French (mean = 755.85ms, SD = 376.74ms) than in English (mean = 805.58ms, SD = 536.91ms) in the neutral condition, while late bilinguals showed the reverse pattern, with a faster reaction time in English (mean = 515.77ms, SD = 196.49ms) than in French (mean = 536.77ms, SD = 204.00ms) in the neutral condition. Therefore overall in the neutral condition, late bilinguals showed faster reaction times in both languages compared to early bilinguals, but early bilinguals performed faster in French and late bilinguals performed faster in English.

Comparing conditions, early bilinguals showed a slightly faster reaction time in the emotion condition in English (English: mean = 817.83ms, SD = 561.08ms) compared to the neutral condition in English (English: mean = 805.58ms, SD = 536.91ms) but showed the reverse pattern in the emotion condition in French (emotion: mean = 726.38ms, SD = 440.26, neutral: mean = 755.85ms, SD = 376.74ms). 

The late bilinguals showed the reverse pattern, demonstrating a slower reaction time in the emotion condition in English (mean = 542.31, SD = 255.28) compared to the neutral condition in English (mean = 515.77ms, SD = 196.49ms), but the same pattern as the early bilinguals across the French conditions, such that they performed slightly faster in the French emotion condition (mean = 533.33ms, SD = 215.51) then the French neutral condition (mean = 536.77ms, SD = 204.00ms), but to a lesser extent than the early bilinguals demonstrated. 

Taken together, late bilinguals had faster reaction times than early bilinguals, contrary to predictions, with early bilinguals perhaps showing an emotional interference effect in the French but not the English condition (where they performed slightly faster in English emotion than English neutral words), and late bilinguals perhaps showing an emotional interference effect in the English condition but not the French condition (where they performed slightly faster in French emotional than French neutral words).

The differing patterns across participant groups in the raw data therefore suggest that the emotional interference effect may differ with age of acquisition, such that late bilinguals overall showed a faster reaction time across both language and condition, suggesting a lack of emotional interference effect, particularly on the French emotion condition, which is their L2. However, raw data results are rather varied, and suggest that any emotional interference effect was not consistent, for example early bilinguals had a stronger interference effect in the neutral condition than the emotion condition. Statistical analysis is required to verify these patterns.

### Plotting the raw data




```{r}
newfinaldata |>
  ggplot(aes(early_late, RT, colour = Language)) +
  geom_jitter(alpha = 0.7, width = 0.1) +
  facet_wrap(~ Condition) +
  labs(
    x = "Early or Late Bilingual", 
    y = "Reaction Time (ms)", 
    title = "AoA (Early or Late Bilingual) influence on reaction time 
    to emotion and to neutral words")
```




#### Figure 1 - Age of acquisition (early or late bilingual) effect on reaction time (RT) across condition (emotion and neutral stimuli) and language (French and English). 

I used a jitter plot to represent the raw data, since it combines numeric (reaction times) and categorical variables. The jitter plot is faceted by Condition (neutral and emotion stimuli), with the x-axis showing Participant group (early or late bilingual) and the y-axis showing Reaction Time (ms). The colours differentiate the Language of the stimuli (French = blue, English = red). 

The plot shows firstly in the emotion condition, that late bilinguals showed less variation and therefore are more densely represented than the early bilinguals. There is minimal variation across both participant groups between languages (English compared to French). 

In the neutral condition, there is slightly more variation across languages, with late bilinguals appearing to perform very slightly faster in English than in French, and early bilinguals' reaction times showing more variation compared to late bilinguals in this condition. The plot reflects a slightly faster reaction time across conditions and across language for late bilinguals.

There are some outliers across condition, group and language, but these appear to be more pronounced in the early bilinguals compared to the late bilinguals.

### Regression modelling

I used Bayesian log-normal regression model for the outcome variable reaction time (RT) using brms, as this distribution allows positive-only values, continuous variables, and is able to account for right-skewed data. This was fitted with Group (early vs late bilinguals), Language (French vs English) and Condition (emotion vs neutral) and their interactions as between-participant fixed effects, and Language (French vs English) and Condition (emotion vs neutral) as within-participant varying slopes, and including Participant ID as by-subject varying intercept to account for inter-participant variability. I then obtained summary measures. As the values are reported as logged reaction time, I used exp() to unlog on the relevant values.




```{r}
brm_newfinaldata <- brm(
  RT ~ early_late * Language * Condition +
    (Language + Condition | id ),
  family = lognormal,
  data = newfinaldata,
  seed = 456,
  cores = 4,
  file = "cache/brmfinaldata", 
  control = list(adapt_delta = 0.99, max_treedepth = 15)
)
```

```{r}
summary(brm_newfinaldata)
```

```{r}
exp(6.17)  #Intercept_95%_lower_bound
exp(6.92)  #Intercept_95%_upper_bound
```




The model did converge, however the summary measures reflect a high degree of uncertainty. 

At 95% credible internal, the model estimates that the mean reaction time for early bilinguals in the emotion and English condition is between 6.17 (lower bound) and 6.92 (upper bound) logged milliseconds, and 478.19ms (lower bound) and 1,0312.32ms (upper bound) unlogged milliseconds. All predictors cross zero at 95% credible internal, and therefore there is a high degree of uncertainty as to their effect size. 




```{r}
summary(brm_newfinaldata, prob = 0.8)
```

```{r}
exp(6.31)  #Intercept_80%_lower_bound
exp(6.76)  #Intercept_80%_upper_bound
```




At 80% credible interval, all predictors still cross zero, but to a lesser extent. The mean reaction time predicted at the Intercept (english_emotion_early) is between 6.31 logged milliseconds and 6.76 logged milliseconds, and 550.04 and 862.64 unlogged milliseconds.  




```{r}
summary(brm_newfinaldata, prob = 0.6)
```

```{r}
exp(6.39)  #Intercept_60%_lower_bound
exp(6.67)  #Intercept_60%_lower_bound
exp(-0.49) #early_lateLatebilingual_60%_lower_bound
exp(-0.09) #early_lateLatebilingual_60%_lower_bound
exp(-0.14) #LanguageFrench_lower_bound
exp(-0.03) #LanguageFrench_lower_bound
```




Finally, at 60% credibility, the predicted reaction time at the intercept is between 6.17 and 6.92 logged milliseconds, or between 595.86 and 788.38 unlogged milliseconds (english_emotion_early). At 60% credibility, two predictors now show a decrease in log milliseconds. Firstly, english_emotion_late shows an effect size between -0.49 and -0.09 (logged) or 0.61 to 0.91 (unlogged), suggesting that late bilinguals are predicted to be faster than early bilinguals in the emotion/English condition. Secondly, french_emotion_early show an effect size between -0.14 and -0.03 (logged) or 0.86 to 0.97 (unlogged), suggesting that early bilinguals in the emotion condition are likely to show a faster reaction time in French than in English. However, the remaining predictors cross zero and thererefore there remains a high degree of uncertainty regarding how these predictors interact across the model. 

After obtaining summary measures at 95%, 80% and 60% credible intervals, I obtained the model draws, implementing the relevant predictors and interactions based on the b_Intercept as english_emotion_early. I then used select() the relevant columns (predictors and their interactions) and pivot_longer() in order to make new columns for these predictors, which I could then plot into the graph (below).




```{r}
draws_brm_newfinaldata <- as_draws_df(brm_newfinaldata)

frame_draws_brm_newfinaldata <- draws_brm_newfinaldata |>
  mutate(
    eng_emotion_early = b_Intercept, 
    fr_emotion_early = b_Intercept + b_LanguageFrench,
    eng_emotion_late = b_Intercept + b_early_lateLatebilingual, 
    eng_neutral_early = b_Intercept + b_ConditionNeutral, 
    fr_emotion_late = b_Intercept + b_early_lateLatebilingual
    + b_LanguageFrench + `b_early_lateLatebilingual:LanguageFrench`,
    eng_neutral_late = b_Intercept + b_ConditionNeutral + 
      b_early_lateLatebilingual + 
      `b_early_lateLatebilingual:ConditionNeutral`,
    fr_neutral_early = b_Intercept + b_LanguageFrench + 
      b_ConditionNeutral +
      `b_LanguageFrench:ConditionNeutral`,
    fr_neutral_late = b_Intercept + b_ConditionNeutral + 
      b_LanguageFrench +
      b_early_lateLatebilingual + 
      `b_early_lateLatebilingual:LanguageFrench` + 
      `b_LanguageFrench:ConditionNeutral` + 
      `b_early_lateLatebilingual:ConditionNeutral` +
      `b_early_lateLatebilingual:LanguageFrench:ConditionNeutral`
  ) |>
  select(eng_emotion_early, fr_emotion_early, eng_emotion_late, 
         eng_neutral_early, fr_emotion_late, eng_neutral_late, 
         fr_neutral_early, fr_neutral_late)

```

```{r}
plot2 <- frame_draws_brm_newfinaldata |>
  select(eng_emotion_early, fr_emotion_early, eng_emotion_late, 
         eng_neutral_early, fr_emotion_late, eng_neutral_late, 
         fr_neutral_early, fr_neutral_late) |>
pivot_longer(
  eng_emotion_early:fr_neutral_late, 
  names_to = "cond", 
  values_to = "epred"
) |>
separate(cond, c("Language", "Condition", "Early_Late"))
  
  
```

```{r}
plot2 |>
  ggplot(aes(epred, Condition, fill = Language )) +
  stat_halfeye(alpha = 0.7) +
  scale_fill_manual(
    labels = c("English", "French"),
    values = c("lightskyblue2", "slateblue1") 
  ) + facet_grid(cols = vars(Early_Late)) +
  labs(title = "Reaction times (logged) across conditions 
       for early and late bilinguals", x = "Reaction times (logged ms)")
  
  
```




#### Figure 2 - Age of acquisition (early or late bilingual) effect on logged reaction time (RT) across condition (emotion and neutral stimuli) and language (French and English). 

I used a half-eye plot to demonstrate logged Reaction times on the x-axis, faceted by Participant group (early, late), and Condition on the y-axis (neutral, emotion), with colour differentiating language (English, French). The plot reflects the model draws, showing very little variability between performance of early and late bilinguals across condition and across language. The most noticeable variation is in the emotion condition for early bilinguals, who appear to show a slightly faster reaction time in French than in English: the model did predict a negative effect on reaction time (i.e. faster reaction time) in these conditions at 60% credibility, however the summary measures showed this to still have a large degree of uncertainty.

## Discussion

The present study investigated if age of acquisition influences reaction time to French and English emotion compared to neutral words in a bilingual Stroop paradigm, comparing the emotional interference effect across early and late bilinguals groups and across languages (French compared to English). 

**Hypothesis 1** predicted that an emotional interference effect would be observed, with both groups showing slower reaction time in the emotion condition compared to the neutral condition. In terms of the overall emotional interference effect, the statistical analysis did not confirm an effect, since the results were highly variable, and therefore no strong conclusions can be drawn.

**Hypothesis 2 and 3** predicted that early bilinguals would display an equal amount of emotional interference across both languages, meaning similar reaction times will be shown, while late bilinguals would show an emotional interference effect (slower reaction time) only in the English condition, as the emotional interference effect is expected to be stronger in the L1.

While the statistical analysis returned highly uncertain results, at 60% credibility it can be predicted that late bilinguals perform faster than early bilinguals in the emotion condition in English, suggesting that an emotional interference effect may be greater for early bilinguals in English. Secondly, also at 60% credibility, early bilinguals are likely to show a faster reaction time in French than in English, suggesting a greater interference effect in English than in French for the early bilinguals. Taken together, this suggests that early bilinguals may show a greater emotional interference effect in English (compared to French, and compared to late bilinguals), which is contrary to hypothesis 2, that they would show an equal interference effect across languages.

However, due to the high uncertainty of results, no conclusions regarding Hypothesis 1, 2, or 3 can be drawn at this stage. In particular, the interactions between age of acquisition, conditions and languages were uncertain, meaning the specific emotional interference effect in L1 compared to L2 is uncertain. Overall therefore, in regards to the research question, the study returned inconclusive results regarding how age of acquisition interacts with the emotional interference effect across L1 and L2.

### Limitations

There are several limitations to the study which could explain the uncertainty of the results.

Firstly, it is still a small participant cohort (5 early bilinguals and 6 late bilinguals), so including more participants may have returned a higher degree of certainty in the results.

Secondly, operationalising age of acquisition as a categorical variable may have obscured between-group differences [@singleton_age_2022; @wagner_what_2022], and therefore treating age of acquisition as a continuous variable may have led to different results.

Additionally, since the main focus of the study was investigating the emotional Stroop effect, rather than the Stroop effect associated with orthographical/colour interference, I chose to include the incorrect trials alongside the correct trials. Removing the incorrect trials may have returned more certainty.

Furthermore, while this study investigated age of acquisition as a predictor of the emotional interference effect across languages, studies reflect that processing of emotion words in L1 and L2 may vary with proficiency, with lower proficiency typically leading to a greater interference effect [@sutton_automatic_2007]. As proficiency in L2 French was not controlled in this study, age of acquisition may have been confounded with proficiency, in particular since the late bilingual group also had a lower proficiency level.

Finally, it is likely that the stimuli presented some difficulties, since the stimuli used by @sutton_automatic_2007 and replicated in this study contain uncommon lexical items (nautical nouns), which are uncommon both in English and in French. It is possible that participants were surprised by these stimuli, meaning that any effect of emotional interference was obscured by a processing delay of encountering an uncommon noun. The late bilingual group, who had lower levels of proficiency in French than the early bilingual group, may also have been completely unfamiliar with these neutral lexical items, which may have conversely affected their reaction times compared to a different set of more common neutral lexical items. It is clear that further investigation is required. 

